body:
Reading this a bit closer this time. It looks like the computation of `interrupt_id` is really just extracting the `INTERRUPT_IDENTITY` field. Let's do this instead:

The `sw_write()` attribute is really expected to be an attribute of the _field_ not the entire register. So, move this inside the definition of the `INTERRUPT_IDENTITY` field and modify it to expect the input to be just the field value:
  ```
      sw_write(csr_value): |
        if (csr_value != 0) {
          clear_imsic_interrupt(PrivilegeMode::S, csr_value);
        }
  ```

Sorry, two things that I forgot: these `sw_write()` functions take the entire CSR value (not just the field), and they need to return the value to be written to the field, so each one needs to change to:
```
  if (csr_value.INTERRUPT_IDENTITY != 0) {
    clear_imsic_interrupt(PrivilegeMode::M, csr_value.INTERRUPT_IDENTITY);
  }
  return csr_value.INTERRUPT_IDENTITY;
```
Apologies for the oversight.

Files Updated:
spec/std/isa/csr/Smaia/mtopei.yaml - Machine Top External Interrupt
spec/std/isa/csr/Ssaia/stopei.yaml - Supervisor Top External Interrupt
spec/std/isa/csr/Ssaia/stopi.yaml - Supervisor Top Interrupt
Changes Made:
Each sw_write() function was updated from:

sw_write(csr_value): |
  if (csr_value != 0) {
    clear_imsic_interrupt(PrivilegeMode::M, csr_value);
  }

To:
sw_write(csr_value): |
  if (csr_value.INTERRUPT_IDENTITY != 0) {
    clear_imsic_interrupt(PrivilegeMode::M, csr_value.INTERRUPT_IDENTITY);
  }
  return csr_value.INTERRUPT_IDENTITY;

